  "id": "minmax-normalization",
  "title": "Implement Min-Max Normalization",
  "description": "Scale numeric data to the interval $[0,1]$. For each slice selected by axis, compute\n\n$$\nx' = \\frac{x - x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max} - x_{\\min}}\n$$\n\nHere, $x_{\\min}$ and $x_{\\max}$ are the minimum and maximum of the same slice. Use column-wise slices when axis=0 and row-wise slices when axis=1. If a slice has range at most eps, return zeros for that slice. Return the scaled values as a NumPy array.",
  "seoDescription": "Normalize each NumPy feature to the zero-to-one range with explicit handling for constant columns.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X = [[1, 2], [3, 6], [5, 10]], axis = 0, eps = 1e-12\n\nOutput: [[0.0, 0.0], [0.5, 0.5], [1.0, 1.0]]\n\nExplanation: Each column is scaled using its own minimum and maximum.",
    "Input: X = [[1, 2], [3, 6], [5, 10]], axis = 1, eps = 1e-12\n\nOutput: [[0.0, 1.0], [0.0, 1.0], [0.0, 1.0]]"
  ],
  "requirements": "* Compute minima and maxima along the selected axis\n* Preserve dimensions during reductions so values broadcast back to X\n* Map every slice with range at most eps to zeros\n* Return a NumPy array of floating-point values",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Implement Min-Max Normalization\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of artificial intelligence. Image pixels, word embeddings, and hidden representations are all structured as multi-dimensional vectors and matrices. **Implement Min-Max Normalization** allows machine learning practitioners to project representations into informative subspaces, rotate coordinate frames, or decompose complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nIn this problem, the objective is to implement **Implement Min-Max Normalization** efficiently and accurately adhering to standard mathematical conventions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe operation is governed by the following mathematical formulation:\n\n$$\nx' = \\frac{x - x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max} - x_{\\min}}\n$$\n\nEvery variable and index in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Pre-processing**: Validate tensor dimensions, convert inputs into contiguous array buffers, and extract structural shape parameters.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Transformation**: Apply vectorized operations directly across array dimensions without explicit Python loops.\n3. **Phase 3: Aggregation & Normalization**: Compute required summations, projections, or element-wise bounds while safeguarding against numerical instability.\n4. **Phase 4: Output Formatting**: Ensure the resulting array or dictionary matches expected output data types and precision tolerances.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Dimensionality & Subspaces**: Orthogonal projections and low-rank factorizations compress massive feature vectors while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Condition Numbers & Inversion**: Inverting ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with near-zero determinants) causes numerical explosion; regularization (e.g. Ridge / Tikhonov) stabilizes these systems.\n* **Tensor Core Acceleration**: Modern hardware accelerators (GPUs and TPUs) are specialized matrix multiplication engines; formulating operations linearly maximizes throughput.",
  "hints": [
    "Use np.min(..., keepdims=True) and np.max(..., keepdims=True) along axis.",
    "Use np.where(data_range > eps, data_range, 1.0) to build a safe denominator."
  ],
  "constraints": "* X is a nonempty one-dimensional or two-dimensional numeric list\n* axis is 0 for one-dimensional inputs and either 0 or 1 for two-dimensional inputs\n* eps is positive\n* Use NumPy only",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef minmax_scale(X: list, axis: int = 0, eps: float = 1e-12) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a floating-point NumPy array matching the shape of X.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Write code here\n    pass",
  "createdAt": "2025-10-21T18:37:15.713Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-03-05T20:18:00.628Z",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nVectorized, numerically stable reference implementation following exact specifications.\n\n### 💻 Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef minmax_scale(X: list, axis: int = 0, eps: float = 1e-12) -> np.ndarray:\n    arr = np.array(X, dtype=float)\n    mn = np.min(arr, axis=axis, keepdims=True)\n    mx = np.max(arr, axis=axis, keepdims=True)\n    return np.round((arr - mn) / (mx - mn + eps), 6)\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the input dimensions.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ for memory buffers.\n",
